{
  "verdict": "REPLICATED",
  "one_line": "Core modular-functor dimensions, braid representations, and empirical density all independently reproduced.",
  "coverage_pct": 90,
  "agreement_pct": 98,
  "per_claim": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PASS",
      "note": "All listed Hilbert-space dimensions V_3^1, V_3^3, V_6^0, V_6^2 match Eq. (4) exactly."
    },
    {
      "id": "C1b",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PASS",
      "note": "Representation-space dimensions for λ=[2,1],[3,3],[4,2] (2,5,8) match the paper’s decomposition."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PASS",
      "note": "All braid generators for λ=[2,1],[3,3],[4,2] are unitary; max deviation ~10^-15 from UU†=I."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PASS",
      "note": "Braid relations and Temperley–Lieb relations satisfied numerically in all sectors with residuals ~10^-15."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PASS",
      "note": "Every generator has spectrum {−1,q} (q=e^{2πi/5}); no extra eigenvalues; multiplicities consistent per i and λ."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PASS",
      "note": "For λ=[4,2], eigenvalue multiplicities are 3 for −1 and 5 for q as in Theorem 3.1(iv)."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "note": "Density theorem itself not numerically provable, but ingredients checked: (a) random braid words in 8-dim sector give broad pairwise distances with many distinct traces (no sign of a small finite subgroup); (b) systematic search of braid words up to length 9 produces steadily improving approximations to a target Hadamard-like SU(2) gate, with Frobenius distance dropping below ~0.14. This is strong empirical support but not a proof of closure containing SU(5)×SU(8)."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "testable": true,
      "status": "FAIL",
      "note": "Direct comparison to the printed ρ_[2,1](σ_2) matrix shows that both plausible readings (A,B) are not unitary, whereas the independently constructed representation is unitary with spectrum {−1,q}. This indicates a likely misprint or convention mismatch in the paper’s explicit matrix; as a standalone numerical claim, the printed matrix itself fails the unitarity sanity check."
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The replication directly recomputes the modular-functor dimensions and representation-space dimensions (C1, C1b), finding exact agreement with the values stated in Freedman–Kitaev–Larsen–Wang. This establishes that the combinatorial and fusion-rule aspects used in the construction were correctly implemented. The Jones representations of the braid group for λ=[2,1],[3,3],[4,2] were then constructed numerically; all tested generators are unitary with machine-precision deviations from UU†=I, and they obey the braid and Temperley–Lieb relations with residuals on the order of 10^-15. These checks reproduce the core algebraic content (C2, C3).\n\nSpectral properties of the braid generators (C4, C5) match the paper’s statements in detail: every generator has eigenvalues exactly {−1,q} with q=e^{2πi/5}, and for λ=[4,2] the multiplicities (3 for −1, 5 for q) agree with Theorem 3.1(iv). These are nontrivial constraints that strongly validate the representation. For the density theorem (C6), the replication sensibly restricts itself to empirical diagnostics: random braid words in the 8-dimensional sector exhibit a broad spread of pairwise distances and many distinct traces, and targeted search for a Hadamard-like gate shows a clear monotone improvement in approximation quality with word length. This is strong numerical evidence that the image is not confined to a small subgroup and behaves as expected under the density theorem, though of course it does not constitute a formal proof.\n\nThe only explicit discrepancy concerns the sanity-check of the printed matrix for ρ_[2,1](σ_2) (C7). The replication reports that both natural readings of the published matrix are non-unitary, while the independently constructed representation is unitary and has the correct eigenvalues {−1,q}. This strongly suggests a misprint or a mismatch of normalization/conventions in the paper’s printed matrix, rather than a failure of the theory, but strictly as a numerical check of the literal printed matrix the claim fails. Overall, however, the core testable content—dimensions, unitarity, braid/TL relations, eigenvalue structure, and numerical signatures of density—has been convincingly reproduced.\n\nGiven the breadth of numerical checks and their close agreement with the theory, the replication merits the verdict REPLICATED. The only caveat is that the density theorem can only be probed heuristically and that the explicit matrix in Section 3 appears inaccurate as printed, but these do not undermine the main numerical claims tested here."
}
